---
code: syntax-malformed
when-fires: the parser hit a structurally malformed sh construct (a parse error); parsing continues fail-soft.
why: inv-no-throw: errors are data. The reason is typed: a SyntaxMalformedReason variant per structural expectation, each with its own prose-component, which the `{reason}` hole renders (28L rul-reason-enums-not-sibling-codes).
owns:
  - syntax-malformed-expected-done-to-close-loop
---
-- book.sh --
#!/bin/sh
# the `for` loop never reaches its `done`
for pkg in nginx redis
do
  apt-get install -y "$pkg"

-- replay --
$ dorc plan --book=book.sh > /dev/null
parse: error[syntax-malformed]: expected `done` to close the loop
      [ book.sh:6:1
   6 | 
     | ^
classify: note[cmdsub-operand-top]: operand 3 is a runtime-dynamic variable
value (unset or branch-conflicted), so Dorc cannot know its value until the
command runs on the host -- there is nothing to resolve and no read-only probe
to check. Dorc elides a command only when it can prove the command's effect is
already in place, so this one is left to run on every apply.
      [ book.sh:5:22
   5 |   apt-get install -y "$pkg"
     |                      \____/

   = repair:  Give the operand a value Dorc can resolve statically -- a literal,
              or a variable assigned from one -- so Dorc can probe it and elide
              the command once it has converged. If the value must stay dynamic,
              load an oracle that vouches for this command's convergence, and
              Dorc will guard the command instead of running it every time.
probe: note[site-unresolvable]: sm 1 site run unprobed (no read-only check could
be shipped): `apt-get install -y "$pkg"` -- run `dorc why` for the per-site
detail (the apply runs each anyway, to stay safe)
      [ book.sh:5:3
   5 |   apt-get install -y "$pkg"
     |   \_______________________/

   = help:  sm site runs `apt-get install -y "$pkg"`
why: ran because operand 3 is a command-substitution `$(...)` or runtime-dynamic value -- its value couldn't be resolved (first seen at 5:3 `apt-get install -y "$pkg"`); so dorc runs it, to stay safe (when unsure, run). to skip it, make the operand a literal Dorc can resolve+probe [resolve-dynamism]
hint: 'apt-get' (line 5) is unmodeled: it is the first wall -- an oracle vouching its convergence would elide it when converged
dorc: run `dorc why` for the per-site cause-chains, or `dorc why book.sh:N` to query a source line
dorc: plan-summary sites=1 elide=0 omit=0 guard=0 run=1 may-alias=0
